Theorem dalloca_pointer_checks_accept[local]:
  EVERY (inst_ptr_ok_check (\name. []) ["p"])
    [mk_inst 0 DALLOCA [Lit 32w] ["p"];
     mk_inst 1 MLOAD [Var "p"] ["v"];
     mk_inst 2 STOP [] []]
Proof
  CONV_TAC EVAL >> simp[]
QED

Theorem dalloca_function_pointer_check_accepts[local]:
  fn_ptr_ok_check (\name. [])
    (mk_raw_function "probe"
      [<|bb_label := "entry"; bb_instructions :=
        [mk_inst 0 DALLOCA [Lit 32w] ["p"];
         mk_inst 1 MLOAD [Var "p"] ["v"];
         mk_inst 2 STOP [] []]|>])
Proof
  CONV_TAC EVAL >> simp[]
QED

(* Interface checkpoint:
   Both examples are safe at every fuel with fmp=0 and unsafe at fuel 1
   after changing ONLY fmp to 32 (frontier stays 32). Thus unrestricted
   endpoint-fmp normalization cannot transfer safety back to the actual
   endpoint. GETFMP is rejected, but the DALLOCA example passes even the
   function-level pointer checker, including its separate pointer roots.
   This is NOT evidence that the function is generated by an accepted
   source program.

   Acceptance chain: lower_vyper_runtime_unit -> run_lowering ->
   lowering_context_ok -> ptr_ok_check -> fn_ptr_ok_check. The latter
   explicitly admits DALLOCA roots (vyperCompiler:416), and its instruction
   checker excludes GETFMP/SETFMP/RETFMP/FMP_PARAM/DRET but not DALLOCA.
   The additional wf, call graph, budget and reachable-block checks have
   not been proved for this hand-written example here.

   Candidate production contract (UNPROVED): for the actual body fn and
   every transitively callable function, exclude all fmp-observing or
   fmp-updating instructions (at least DALLOCA, GETFMP, SETFMP, RETFMP,
   FMP_PARAM, DRET); then prove
     (!fuel. mem_safe_run fuel ctx fn (out with vs_fmp := chosen)) ==>
     (!fuel. mem_safe_run fuel ctx fn out).
   A call-sensitive simulation is required, not merely field equality.
   The generic implication without restrictions is disproved above.
   The pointer checker supplies the five non-DALLOCA exclusions but NOT
   DALLOCA exclusion. Lowering-wide DALLOCA exclusion remains missing,
   as does the restricted simulation itself. No claim that normalization
   solves the frozen existential theorem is made by these probes.
*)
